{"answers":[{"question_id":"Q009_heme_bundle_fold_not_homology","answer":"No. SCOPe 2.08 does not support treating these three groups as one homologous superfamily. It places all three in the same fold, f.21 "Heme-binding four-helical bundle" (sunid 81344, class f, membrane and cell surface proteins), whose core is defined purely architecturally: four transmembrane helices in an up-and-down bundle binding one or two heme groups between the helices. Within that fold they are three separate superfamilies: f.21.1 Transmembrane di-heme cytochromes (sunid 81342), f.21.2 Fumarate reductase respiratory complex transmembrane subunits (sunid 81343), and f.21.3 Respiratory nitrate reductase 1 gamma chain (sunid 103501, mapped to Pfam PF02665). In SCOPe's hierarchy, a superfamily asserts probable common ancestry, whereas a shared fold only asserts the same major secondary-structure arrangement and topology, which may arise by convergence, especially for a simple, physically favored architecture such as a heme-sandwiching transmembrane helical bundle. The curators' placement at separate superfamilies therefore means they judged the evidence insufficient for homology.

The node comments reinforce this. Within f.21.1, SCOPe notes that three of the four heme ligands are conserved between its two families and that the hemes bind similarly, which is the kind of positional and functional-site evidence used to justify homology at the superfamily level; no such statement is made across the three superfamilies. For f.21.2, the comment notes the common fold is formed by a single chain in one family and by two chains in the other. For f.21.3, the comment says the nitrate reductase gamma subunit is a "possible link between the two other superfamilies": it corresponds functionally to the gamma subunit of formate dehydrogenase N but is structurally closer to fumarate reductase subunit FrdC. That is explicitly a hypothesis of a possible relationship, not an assertion of homology, and SCOPe kept it as a distinct superfamily.

Practical limits: (1) Do not transfer function, heme-ligand positions, electron-transfer mechanism, or quinone-binding sites between these superfamilies on the basis of fold membership alone; such transfer is only reasonable within a superfamily or family. (2) Do not use a member of one superfamily as a homology-modeling template for another on the strength of SCOPe classification; fold-level similarity does not guarantee alignable sequence or conserved core residues. (3) Establishing homology across these superfamilies would require external evidence beyond SCOPe, such as significant sequence or profile-HMM similarity (e.g., Pfam clan membership linking PF02665 to the other families), conserved heme-ligating histidine positions on equivalent helices, shared unusual structural features beyond the generic bundle, or phylogenetic/genomic context linking the respiratory complexes. (4) Any future SCOPe release could merge them if such evidence emerges, so the conclusion is release-specific to 2.08.","evidence_used":["mcp__scope__get_scop_node","mcp__scope__list_scop_children","/api/v1/releases/current","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sccs/f.21","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sccs/f.21/parents","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sccs/f.21/children?limit=20","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/81344","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/81344/annotations","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/81342","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/81342/annotations","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/81343","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/81343/annotations","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/103501","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/103501/annotations"]}]}
